public class LoginServlet extends HttpServlet {
    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) {
        String user = request.getParameter("username");
        String pass = request.getParameter("password");
        // Vulnerable: SQL injection due to concatenating user input
        String query = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '" + user + "' AND password = '" + pass + "'";
        try {
            Statement stmt = connection.createStatement();
            ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery(query);
            if (rs.next()) {
                // login successful
            }
        } catch (SQLException e) { ... }
    }
}

Then the fixed code would use PreparedStatement:

public class LoginServlet extends HttpServlet {
    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) {
        String user = request.getParameter("username");
        String pass = request.getParameter("password");
        String query = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ? AND password = ?";
        try {
            PreparedStatement pstmt = connection.prepareStatement(query);
            pstmt.setString(1, user);
            pstmt.setString(2, pass);
            ResultSet rs = pstmt.executeQuery();
            if (rs.next()) {
                // login successful
            }
        } catch (SQLException e) { ... }
    }
}

Yes, that shows the vulnerability and the fix. The comments in the bad code mention "vulnerable" and SQL injection. The good code uses parameterized queries. That should work.

